## Why This Is Asked
This question tests per-key stateful processing, TTL-based windowing, and idempotence across replays—core KCNA concepts—while exercising cross-event aggregation and emission semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key in-memory or stateful session store with a 30-second TTL
- Idempotent emits using a deterministic sessionId (userId:start)
- Out-of-order arrivals handled via lastEventTs and session boundaries
- At-least-once delivery guaranteed by committing offsets only after emit

## Code Example
```javascript
function onMessage(ctx, msg) {
  const session = sessionStore().getOrCreate(msg.userId, () => ({
    startTs: msg.timestamp,
    endTs: msg.timestamp,
    count: 0,
    lastEventTs: msg.timestamp
  }));
  session.endTs = msg.timestamp;
  session.lastEventTs = msg.timestamp;
  session.count++;
  sessionStore().setTtl(msg.userId, 30000, () => {
    ctx.emit('sessions', {
      userId: msg.userId,
      sessionStart: session.startTs,
      sessionEnd: session.endTs,
      eventCount: session.count
    });
    sessionStore().remove(msg.userId);
  });
}

function commitOffsets() {
  ctx.commit();
}

function sessionStore() {
  return ctx.state();
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced fairness and isolation in a multi-tenant KCNA cluster; demonstrates practical reliability under bursty traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant rate limiting (token bucket, leaky bucket)
- Global throughput vs. per-tenant isolation
- Distributed state store for buckets (Redis, etc.)
- Backpressure mechanisms and producer signaling
- Canary strategy and measurable success criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// simplified token bucket sketch
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }

  allow(n = 1) {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.lastRefill = now;
  }
}

// Global limiter wrapping per-tenant buckets
class TenantRateController {
  constructor(tenants, globalRate) {
    this.buckets = new Map();
    this.globalLimiter = new TokenBucket(globalRate, globalRate * 2);
    for (const [id, quota] of tenants) {
      this.buckets.set(id, new TokenBucket(quota.rate, quota.burst));
    }
  }

  tryProcess(tenantId, n = 1) {
    const bucket = this.buckets.get(tenantId);
    if (!bucket || !bucket.allow(n)) return false;
    if (!this.globalLimiter.allow(n)) {
      bucket.tokens += n; // rollback tenant token
      return false;
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to apply backpressure and lag-based throttling in a practical Kafka scenario, evaluating skills in observability, dynamic resource tuning, and safe processing under bursty workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Per-partition lag measurement with rolling window statistics
- Dynamic throttling and in-flight request control
- Canary-style test design with simulated traffic bursts
- Observability: lag metrics, throughput monitoring, and alert thresholds

## Code Example
```javascript
class LagController {
  constructor(targetLag = 1000, windowSize = 10) {
    this.targetLag = targetLag;
    this.windowSize = windowSize;
    this.lagWindow = new Map(); // partition -> lag samples[]
    this.inFlight = new Map();  // partition -> current in-flight permits
  }

  recordLag(partition, lag) {
    if (!this.lagWindow.has(partition)) {
      this.lagWindow.set(partition, []);
    }
    const samples = this.lagWindow.get(partition);
    samples.push(lag);
    if (samples.length > this.windowSize) samples.shift();
  }

  getAverageLag(partition) {
    const samples = this.lagWindow.get(partition) || [];
    return samples.length ? samples.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / samples.length : 0;
  }

  shouldThrottle(partition) {
    return this.getAverageLag(partition) > this.targetLag;
  }

  adjustThrottle(partition) {
    const avgLag = this.getAverageLag(partition);
    const currentInFlight = this.inFlight.get(partition) || 10;

    if (avgLag > this.targetLag) {
      // Reduce fetch rate: decrease in-flight permits
      const reduced = Math.max(1, Math.floor(currentInFlight * 0.8));
      this.inFlight.set(partition, reduced);
    } else {
      // Ramp up slowly when lag is healthy
      const increased = Math.min(100, Math.floor(currentInFlight * 1.2));
      this.inFlight.set(partition, increased);
    }
    return this.inFlight.get(partition);
  }
}

// Minimal canary test
async function canaryTest() {
  const controller = new LagController(1000);
  const partitions = [0, 1, 2, 3];

  // Simulate burst: spike lag across all partitions
  for (const p of partitions) {
    controller.recordLag(p, 5000); // burst above target
  }

  // Verify throttling triggers
  const allThrottled = partitions.every(p => controller.shouldThrottle(p));
  console.log(`Throttle triggered: ${allThrottled}`);

  // Adjust and verify permits decrease
  partitions.forEach(p => {
    const permits = controller.adjustThrottle(p);
    console.log(`Partition ${p}: permits = ${permits}`);
  });
}
```

## Expected Answer Elements
- **Data structures**: Lag sample window per partition, in-flight permit tracker, target threshold
- **Triggers**: Timer-based lag sampling, configurable poll interval, lag spike detection
- **Canary test**: Burst generator simulating traffic spike, assertion that lag stabilizes within target

